{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The correct pattern ALREADY EXISTS in this repo — adopt it rather than invent one\n\n`~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh` (the daily worktree GC LaunchAgent) solved exactly this hazard\nalready, and its own header says why:\n\n> Tier B was added 2026-08-06. Tier A alone is too strict for this repo: `main` is\n> Rebase-If-Necessary, so Gerrit rewrites SHAs on submit and a [branch that landed is no longer an\n> ancestor].\n\nIts safe-to-remove predicate is two-tier:\n\n- **Tier A** — HEAD is a true ancestor of `origin/main` (literally on main). This is the\n  `is-ancestor` test `feature-branch-inventory.sh:81` uses ALONE, and the GC's own comment states\n  it is insufficient for this repo.\n- **Tier B** — every file the branch touched since its merge-base is **BYTE-IDENTICAL** to\n  `origin/main`'s version of that file.\n\nTier B catches precisely the case this bug is about: a change that landed under a rewritten SHA is\nno longer an ancestor, but its content is provably in main. Observed working in today's 04:00 run:\n\n```\nREMOVED fix-scanner-integration-90c7: no unlanded bytes (tier A), clean, idle\nREMOVED fix-tf-fmt-9ea3:              no unlanded bytes (tier B), clean, idle\n```\n\nBoth tiers firing on the same run, on real branches.\n\n## What this changes for AC2\n\nAC2 asks the detection to distinguish merged-via-rebase from genuinely-not-in-main. There is now a\nproven in-repo implementation of that distinction, so the work is ADOPTION rather than design.\n\nIt also settles the open question this ticket recorded — whether `Change-Id` lookup suffices or\npatch-id equivalence is also needed. Byte-identity of touched files is STRONGER than a `Change-Id`\nmatch and does not depend on the trailer being present or preserved, so it answers the case I\nflagged as unproven (an amended patchset never submitted in that exact form, with an equivalent\ncommit landing separately): content equality holds there, ancestry and `Change-Id` may not.\n\nNote the asymmetry that makes Tier B safe in the GC's context and worth preserving here: it can\nreport \"no unlanded bytes\" for a branch whose commits differ from main, which is exactly the\ndesired answer, but it never reports unlanded work as landed — it compares against main's actual\nfile content.\n\n## Suggested revision to the fix direction\n\nRather than adding a `Change-Id` lookup to `feature-branch-inventory.sh`, port the GC's two-tier\npredicate: keep `is-ancestor` as the cheap fast path (MERGED-BACK), fall back to byte-identity of\ntouched-since-merge-base files (MERGED-REBASED), and report NOT-IN-MAIN only when both fail. That\nyields the three-state output AC2 asks for, using an implementation already exercised daily on this\nhost against ~169 worktrees.\n\nCross-reference the GC script in the change so the two implementations are discoverable from each\nother; a future divergence between them is the next instance of this bug."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788645429271388001,"uuid":"80f97a32-87aa-4d0d-965a-2925b96b55b3"}